From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 06:59:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] virtual: fail a W+X request that mprotect granted without
 WRITE

mprotect_exec's fast path returned success as soon as mprotect granted
EXEC, even when the caller had also asked for WRITE and the kernel had
dropped it (the ml999 report). The caller was told it had writable memory
and faulted again on its next store.

No title reached that report: none of the phone logs of Hollow Knight,
The Witcher 3 and En Garde! has it, and on a TXM phone mprotect never
grants EXEC, so a W+X request takes the JIT-pool path, which serves it
through the pool's RW and RX aliases. A request that does reach the fast
path now fails with EACCES, so NtProtectVirtualMemory returns
STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED instead of a success whose WRITE is gone. The pages
keep the RX protection mprotect gave them, and the launch's `limits:` line
counts the request (wx_dropped).

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-09-28-runtime-limits.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c | 25 ++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c
index ce1041b..3085ffe 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/virtual_ios.c
@@ -9635,20 +9635,23 @@ static inline int mprotect_exec( void *base, size_t size, int unix_prot )
                  * retry loop she warned about, after the classification fix
                  * removes the first one.
                  *
-                 * Report only; do NOT change the return here. Whether the right
-                 * answer is to fail the request, to drop EXEC and keep WRITE
-                 * (the ml957 "backing stays non-exec, pool copy keeps X"
-                 * policy), or to leave it to the caller depends on who asked,
-                 * and that is the next run's discriminator rather than a guess. */
+                 * Playport: fail it. No title reached this report
+                 * (docs/evidence/2026-09-28-runtime-limits.md): on a TXM phone
+                 * mprotect never grants EXEC, so a W+X request takes the JIT-pool
+                 * path below, which serves it through the pool's RW and RX
+                 * aliases. A request that does reach here now fails with EACCES,
+                 * so NtProtectVirtualMemory returns STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED instead
+                 * of a success whose WRITE is gone, and the launch's `limits:`
+                 * line counts it (wx_dropped). */
                 if ((unix_prot & PROT_WRITE) && !(info.protection & VM_PROT_WRITE))
                 {
-                    static unsigned wsurv;
-                    __sync_fetch_and_add( &ios_limit_wx_dropped, 1 );
-                    if (wsurv++ < 16)
+                    unsigned long n = __sync_add_and_fetch( &ios_limit_wx_dropped, 1 );
+                    if (n <= 16 || !(n % 256))
                         dprintf( 2, "ml999: mprotect_exec %p+%#lx asked for W+X but only "
-                                 "prot=%#x survived -- returning SUCCESS with WRITE DROPPED. "
-                                 "A caller expecting writable backing will fault again\n",
-                                 base, (unsigned long)size, info.protection );
+                                 "prot=%#x survived -- FAILING the request (#%lu)\n",
+                                 base, (unsigned long)size, info.protection, n );
+                    errno = EACCES;
+                    return -1;
                 }
                 return 0;  /* genuinely RX/RWX — done */
             }
